4193115234375, -0.0132904052734375, -0.02581787109375, 0.0024890899658203125, -0.021820068359375, -0.015045166015625, 0.02642822265625, -0.0087890625, 0.005157470703125, 0.0294189453125, 0.02105712890625, 0.0269775390625, 0.005245208740234375, -0.0211181640625, -0.0222015380859375, -0.00525665283203125, -0.00496673583984375, 0.019317626953125, 0.01369476318359375, -0.00670623779296875, -0.01473236083984375, 0.005290985107421875, 0.0157623291015625, 0.0269317626953125, -0.003437042236328125, -0.018096923828125, -0.0204010009765625, 0.0312042236328125, 0.0019350051879882812, -0.0010614395141601562, 0.00029468536376953125, -0.0095977783203125, -0.0029811859130859375, 0.01454925537109375, 0.00832366943359375, -0.0038890838623046875, -0.01308441162109375, -0.0028076171875, -0.003940582275390625, -0.02374267578125, -0.0022029876708984375, 0.0273895263671875, -0.00827789306640625, 0.005214691162109375, -0.01122283935546875, 0.002986907958984375, 0.01416778564453125, -0.00788116455078125, 0.005279541015625, -0.004791259765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Methods**\n\n*Symbolic Pattern Construction and Tie Encoding*:  \n**Step 1: Algorithmic Procedure**  \nAt each time point, we construct a symbolic ordinal pattern for each oscillator by ranking its instantaneous phase \u03b8i relative to its spatial neighbors, as defined by the network adjacency. The local phase vector is sorted in ascending order.  \n- If the absolute phase difference |\u03b8i\u2212\u03b8j| between any two elements is less than the phase tie threshold \u03b4\u03b8, those elements are marked as tied (denoted by \u2018=\u2019) in their ordinal encoding, replacing strict ordinal distinctions.  \n- Example: For a local set [\u03b81, \u03b82, \u03b83] with \u03b81 < \u03b82 \u2248 \u03b83 and |\u03b82\u2013\u03b83| < \u03b4\u03b8, the pattern is encoded as \u20181=2\u2019, meaning \u03b81 < \u03b82 = \u03b83 in rank positions.\n\n**Step 2: Interpretive Rationale**  \nTie symbols within ordinal patterns directly mark clusters of oscillators with nearly identical phases\u2014defining phase plateaus or local synchrony.  \n- Consecutive tie symbols represent spatially contiguous regions sharing phase.  \n- With ties included, the variety of symbolic patterns grows, capturing strictly ordered patterns (traveling waves, monotonic gradients), local clusters (plateaus), or mixed arrangements.  \n- Examples: Pattern types are summarized and illustrated visually in Supplementary Figure 1A\u2013C; key characteristics are summarized here:  \n    - 1A: Strictly increasing pattern (traveling wave/gradient)  \n    - 1B: Tied elements indicating phase plateau  \n    - 1C: Patterns with local clusters or heterogeneous motifs  \nThe empirical distribution of these patterns forms the basis of the spatial permutation entropy, computed as the Shannon entropy of observed symbolic patterns.\n\n*Phase Difference Threshold Selection (\u03b4\u03b8)*:  \n**Selection Approach**  \nTo determine \u03b4\u03b8, we performed a parameter sweep across \u03b4\u03b8 \u2208 [0.05, 0.2] radians. For each \u03b4\u03b8, we computed:  \n- (1) The variance of spatial permutation entropy within each dynamical regime,  \n- (2) The balanced classification accuracy for regime identification, using entropy as the input feature for a supervised classifier (random forest, sklearn 1.1.1, 100 trees, default parameters).\n\n**Quantitative Results**  \n- Regime classification accuracy and within-regime entropy variance were plotted as a function of \u03b4\u03b8 (Supplementary Figure 2A-B; summarized here: accuracy (95% CI) and variance minima).  \n    - In synthetic data, regime classification peaked at \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians (accuracy = 0.92 [0.89, 0.95] for chimera detection). Accuracy dropped outside \u03b4\u03b8 \u2208 [0.08, 0.13].  \n    - In EEG, classification accuracy plateaued in \u03b4\u03b8 \u2208 [0.09, 0.12] (accuracy = 0.79 [0.76, 0.83]).  \n    - Entropy variance was minimized at \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians in both data types.  \nWe fixed \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians as the setting best balancing low within-regime entropy variance against high regime classification accuracy.  \n- As a practical guideline, \u03b4\u03b8 should be set at or just above the estimated standard deviation of noise-driven phase fluctuations.  \n- Where no ground truth exists, we recommend plotting both entropy variance and classification metrics versus \u03b4\u03b8 (examples in Supplementary Figure 2A-B) to guide selection.\n\n*Neighborhood Definition and Parameterization*:  \n**(A) Synthetic Data (Rings and Grids):**  \n- *Topology*: For ring and 2D grid networks, each node\u2019s neighborhood includes its k immediate spatial neighbors: default k = 2 (i\u20131, i+1 for rings; four orthogonal neighbors for grids).\n- *Border Handling*: Nodes at network borders include only existing adjacent nodes (no padding or periodic wrapping).\n- *Expanded Neighborhoods*: For larger k (k = 3 or 4), next-nearest neighbors are included; border neighborhoods truncated accordingly.\n\n**(B) EEG Data:**  \n- *Adjacency Definition*: For each electrode, neighbors were first assigned based on direct physical neighbors in the 10-20 EEG layout.\n- *Montage Variations and Missing Channels*: For missing or border electrodes, only available neighbors were included; no virtual connections were added.\n- *Irregular or High-Density Layouts*: For nonstandard or high-density caps, adjacency lists were generated by assigning all electrodes within a 4-cm Euclidean radius as neighbors. This proximity threshold captured 3\u20135 adjacent electrodes per channel (matching the spatial density of standard 10-20 layouts; see Supplementary Table 1 for adjacency matrices).\n- *Software*: Network construction used MNE-Python 1.2, with custom scripts for spatial adjacency.\n\n**Choice and Sensitivity of k**  \nFor EEG, k was selected to reflect physical electrode density while balancing local pattern detectability and noise.  \n- We tested k \u2208 [2, 3, 4, 5]; classification accuracy (as above) was robust for k = 3\u20135 (\u0394accuracy < 0.02; Supplementary Figure 2C).  \n- k = 3 was used for standard 10-20 layouts; for denser or irregular arrays, k was scaled so that the proportion of neighbor channels per electrode matched 10-20 (<20%).  \n- The effect of varying k is quantified: accuracy remained above 0.79 for all k in the tested range. Thus, results are not highly sensitive to neighborhood size in the practical range examined.\n\n**(C) Irregular/Complex Networks:**  \n- *Automated Construction*: Used node coordinates (when available) to define neighbors as all nodes within a set spatial radius or fixed degree; neighbor sets were checked visually for network anatomical plausibility.\n- *Normalization*: For networks with variable degree, we normalized local ordinal pattern counts to ensure entropy did not artificially drop due to differing neighborhood sizes (see Discussion: Limitations).\n\n**Experimental Design and Validation**\n\n*Synthetic Oscillator Simulations*:  \n- *Simulation Setup*: N = 100 Kuramoto oscillators arranged in rings or 10\u00d710 grids, with variable coupling (0.05\u20131.0), intrinsic frequency spread (\u03c3 = 0.02\u20130.2), and noise amplitude (\u03b7 = 0.01\u20130.1). Regimes were induced by adjusting parameters or applying controlled perturbations.\n- *Operational Regime Labels*: Precise regime definitions and thresholds are given in Supplementary Table 2 (see also Methods Appendix):\n    - Synchrony: Global order parameter R > 0.9; spatial phase SD < 0.1 (\u2265200 time steps)\n    - Chimera: 0.5 < R < 0.7; cluster index > 0.3 (see Methods Appendix for definition)\n    - Traveling Wave: R \u2248 0.8; mean local phase difference > 0.15; consistent spatial phase gradient  \n  Regime designations were confirmed by two blinded raters on random samples (n = 20 per regime; interrater agreement \u03ba = 0.92).\n\n- *Dataset*: Each regime comprised 50 independent simulations (10,000 time steps each).\n\n*Regime Transition Detection and Statistical Analysis*:  \n- *Entropy Computation*: Calculated spatial permutation entropy in overlapping windows of 250 steps (50% overlap; i.e., 125-step increments).\n- *Cross-Validation and ROC Analysis*: Used stratified 5-fold cross-validation, ensuring all windows from a given simulation fell entirely within one fold to avoid temporal leakage.  \n- *Classification*: Random forest classifier (100 trees, max_depth=8, balanced class weights) fit to the training set, tested on held-out fold.\n- *ROC Generation*: Predicted regime labels (entropy-based) were compared to ground truth for each window to yield ROC curves.\n- *Statistical Significance*: Area under ROC curve (AUC) and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals computed with 1,000 stratified bootstrap samples. Where multiple p-values were computed, Bonferroni-corrected thresholds were used to control for multiple comparisons. Example: For chimera detection, mean AUC = 0.93 [0.91, 0.95], p < 0.001. Phase coherence-based classifier yielded AUC = 0.67 [0.62, 0.72] (Supplementary Figure 2C summarizes parameter sweeps and performance).\n- *Limitations*: Edge nodes with fewer neighbors can reduce pattern diversity and bias local entropy downward. Normalization and confirmation analyses (Supplementary Figure 4) confirm these effects are minor for typical topologies; we recommend caution on heavily degree-varying graphs.\n\n**Application to EEG Data**\n\n*Preprocessing and Neighborhood Assignment*:  \nEEG datasets included 15 healthy participants, each contributing 10 eyes-open and 10 eyes-closed recordings (60 s/session; 32 channels). Processing steps:  \n- Bandpass filter: 8\u201313 Hz (alpha), 4th order zero-phase Butterworth filter.  \n- Re-referencing: Linked ears.  \n- Segmentation: Non-overlapping 2-s epochs.  \n- Artifact rejection: Any epoch exceeding amplitude (|\u03bc| > 100 \u03bcV) or kurtosis (z > 6) thresholds (Delorme & Makeig, 2004) was removed\u2014on average, 8.7% (amplitude) and 3.5% (kurtosis) of epochs per subject.  \n- Adjacency: For standard 10-20 layouts, physical neighbors as above; for incomplete/irregular montages, neighbor lists constructed via a 4-cm spatial radius using registered electrode positions (see Supplementary"}}